title: Value picker
tags: GUI
kind: reference
aliases: value-picker picker pick
summary: To fill a reference, a form opens a throwaway workspace in the other slot: browse it with the ordinary panels, then confirm the highlighted metarecord.

Typing a metarecord's uuid to fill a `ref` or a `tree_ref` is impractical. The
🔍 button of a value field ([[Field editor]]) opens a ''picker'' instead: an ordinary
workspace, made for the occasion, in which you find the metarecord with the usual
panels and keys, then hand it back to the form.

!! Picking

* The picker opens in the ''other'' slot, and takes the focus; the form stays visible. Its status bar says what to choose.
* For a `ref` it is a `metarecord-list`, with the finder focused: type to narrow. It starts on the query configured for the field, if there is one ([[Ref value seeds]]). For a `tree_ref` it is the [[treeref panel]], on the field's own tree.
* Move as usual: highlighting a row commits nothing.
* <<key "pick:confirm" "ctrl+enter">> confirms; <<key "pick:cancel" "ctrl+escape">> gives up. Closing the picker like any workspace gives up too.

Either way the picker disappears, the slot shows again what it showed before — or
is hidden again — and the focus returns to the form.

The two keys are chords so that plain `Enter` and `Escape` keep their meaning in
the picker's panels. Confirming with nothing highlighted is refused, and the picker
stays open.

!! What comes back

* For a `ref`, the uuid is the value.
* For a `tree_ref`, the uuid is the ''parent''; the name remains to be typed. Picking `/document/test` and typing `report` gives `/document/test/report`.

A picker can also return a ''path'': the [[repos panel]]'s //Browse…// opens the
[[file-manager panel]] on the disk, and the folder highlighted there comes back.

!! How it works

A thin protocol over [[Workspace variables]]. The form calls
`metafolder.pick.start(spec)`, and decides everything about its picker, since only
it knows what the picker is for:

|!`spec` |!Meaning |
|`token` |any value, returned with the answer: it tells the form which of its fields asked |
|`panel` |`{type, vars}` — the panel type to show, and the workspace variables to start it with (a query, a tree field) |
|`repo` |the picker's repository |
|`name` |the picker workspace's name |
|`prompt` |the text shown in its status bar |
|`result` |`uuid` (the default): the picker's `selected_metarecord`. `path`: the first of its `selected_paths` |

The GUI composes it from the workspace primitives ([[Workspaces]]): it creates an
''empty'' workspace — not a copy of the caller's, whose own list state (a frozen query)
would otherwise run instead of the seed — sends it the panel's variables and
`pick_request` — who asked, the token, which slot it took and what that slot showed —
and shows it in the slot other than the caller's. A picker is not forked
([[workspace:fork]]).

On confirmation it reads the selection, closes the picker, restores the slot and
sends `pick_result` to the ''caller's'' workspace: `{token, uuid}` or
`{token, path}`, and `{token, cancelled: true}` for a cancellation. Like every write
across workspaces, it goes through `workspace:send` — a panel's `workspace` reads and
writes the variables of its own workspace only.

`createPickRunner` of `/__value-widget.js` wraps the exchange in a promise.
